---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Glossary · San Lee"
  description="Terms used on this site, each defined as it is used here and tied to a decision."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/glossary.html"
  ogType="website"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Used-here plate. Textbook vs this system's meaning. No oxide:
       this is a glossary, not a reversal.

       The plate now sits in the MONOGRAPH figure ladder (ADR-014). The
       wrapper is the .fig fig--plate div, so style.css owns the width cap
       (--fig-plate), the night ground, the pocket edition, and the owned
       margin. The four declarations that stated those here are DELETED
       rather than out-cascaded, per the ladder's own rule. What stays is
       page-scoped: the stroke, the fill, and the type inside this one
       drawing. */
    /* The box stroke reads --stroke-figure, not --rule-strong (ADR-014,
       amendment A2). The two tokens are the same hex in light. In dark the
       figure token is #b0a591 against the global rule's #6b6252, so the
       night retune reaches the plate and leaves every rule outside the
       figure layer at its July value. I0 moved the two prototype pages'
       plates and left each remaining page's plate to that page's lane.
       fill: var(--bg) is deliberate and unchanged: --bg is scoped to
       --fig-ground inside a dark figure, so the knockout tracks the lifted
       ground instead of punching a darker hole in it. */
    .gloss-plate .gp-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--stroke-figure);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .gloss-plate .gp-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .gloss-plate .gp-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    /* .gp-micro is DELETED. No <text> node on this page ever carried it, so
       it drew nothing; it declared 8px, which the microtext floor now
       forbids (CLAUDE.md, ADR-014 section 5). A dead below-floor rule is a
       trap for the next editor of this plate. */
    .gloss-plate .gp-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .gloss-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    /* The caption now sits INSIDE the figure wrapper, so the PL-2 ladder in
       style.css does not reach it: the ladder owns direct children of
       main.wrap only. The old bottom margin is gone; the wrapper owns the
       air under the figure now.

       THE GEOMETRY IS NO LONGER RESTATED HERE. style.css carries
       `.fig--plate > p`, which gives every bare-plate caption the prose
       measure and the centring; that is the ask PR #286 left for the one hand
       on that file, now taken. Without it this caption inherits the wrapper's
       promoted wide track and runs 884px at 1280px against a 544px prose
       measure. This caption still cannot BE a <figcaption> — one outside a
       <figure> is invalid, and a <figure> here fails the figure contract —
       which is exactly why the shared selector reaches for the <p>.

       What stays local is the voice. The chrome pages caption in `--muted` at
       0.92rem; the project pages use the note voice. One shared selector
       cannot decide that without changing what a page renders. */
    .gloss-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="glossary" />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="index.html">&larr; Back</a>
    <h1>Glossary</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Terms used on this site, each defined as it is used here and tied to a
      decision.
    </p>
    <!-- The plate takes its number and its kind in the five-kind vocabulary
         (ADR-014, PL-3): a real span with a hand-authored digit, in flow,
         one line above the artwork, at every width. It is the only figure on
         this page, so it is Plate 01. The wrapper is a div and never a
         <figure>: figure-contract.cjs fails a <figure> that carries no
         data-fig, and a data-fig figure owes two caption slots this
         one-slot caption does not have. -->
    <div class="fig fig--plate">
    <span class="fig-label">Plate 01</span>
    <svg class="gloss-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 72" role="img"
         aria-label="These are not textbook definitions. Each term is defined the way it was used in this system, and why it mattered here.">
      <title>Textbook meaning on the left. How I used it here on the right.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="gp-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>
      <text class="gp-lane" x="4" y="12">TEXTBOOK</text>
      <rect class="gp-box" x="4" y="18" width="110" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="gp-title" x="59" y="40" text-anchor="middle">generic def</text>
      <path class="gp-edge" d="M 114 36 L 134 36" marker-end="url(#gp-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <text class="gp-lane" x="138" y="12">HERE</text>
      <rect class="gp-box" x="138" y="18" width="118" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="gp-title" x="197" y="40" text-anchor="middle">why it mattered</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="gloss-plate-caption">
      Filter the list. Each entry points at a decision on this site.
    </p>
    </div>

    <input
      type="search"
      id="glossary-filter"
      class="glossary-filter"
      placeholder="Filter terms&hellip;"
      aria-label="Filter glossary terms"
      autocomplete="off"
    />
    <p id="glossary-empty" class="muted-line" hidden>No terms match.</p>

    <div class="glossary-list">
      <div class="decision" id="term-adr" data-term="adr sys architecture decision record">
        <p class="tag">Convention</p>
        <h3>ADR / SYS-NNN</h3>
        <p>I write one of these for every decision worth defending later:
          context, choice, tradeoff, alternatives considered. Numbered so I
          can cite it instead of re-arguing it.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> <code>ADR-NNN</code> are
          decisions scoped to one repo; <code>SYS-NNN</code> are the ones that
          cross repo boundaries &mdash; the contracts wiring notes-api, the
          classifier, and kb-agent together.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-agent" data-term="agent kb-agent">
        <p class="tag">AI</p>
        <h3>Agent</h3>
        <p>Software that chains tool calls toward a goal instead of answering
          in one shot: pick a tool, read the result, decide the next step,
          repeat until done or stuck.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> kb-agent is mine &mdash;
          it searches the knowledge base and synthesizes a grounded answer
          instead of guessing from what the model already knows.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-backgroundtask" data-term="backgroundtask fastapi kafka async">
        <p class="tag">ARCH</p>
        <h3>BackgroundTask</h3>
        <p>A way to run a follow-up step right after a request without
          standing up a message queue: the caller gets a response
          immediately, and the extra work runs in the same process a moment
          later.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> notes-api uses FastAPI's
          <code>BackgroundTask</code> to call the classifier and write its
          labels back as tags. I first built this loop on Kafka, then
          right-sized it once the workload didn't earn a broker.
          <a href="projects/the-system.html#decision-rightsized">Full decision &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-baseline" data-term="baseline bake-off tf-idf sklearn logistic regression">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>Baseline</h3>
        <p>The simplest credible alternative, measured first, so the fancier
          approach has a number to beat instead of a vibe.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> I built a TF-IDF +
          logistic-regression baseline and ran it against the LLM classifier
          on the same gold rows. The LLM won decisively &mdash; and the losing
          baseline now runs live in your browser on the demo page, so the
          comparison is inspectable rather than asserted.
          <a href="projects/classifier-baseline.html">Full demo &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-bm25" data-term="bm25 keyword search lexical retrieval">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>BM25</h3>
        <p>A sparse keyword-retrieval method: it scores documents by how
          often and how distinctively they contain your query terms, no
          model required.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> I tested it as a
          lightweight grounding layer for the classifier. The measured lift
          (+1.9% category accuracy, a net of one call in 54, and +0.0% on
          domain) didn't justify the added complexity, so it didn't ship.
          <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html#decision-grounding">Full decision &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-classification" data-term="classification classifier label">
        <p class="tag">AI</p>
        <h3>Classification</h3>
        <p>Sorting text into a fixed set of labels using a model, instead of
          hand-written rules.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> my classifier assigns three
          labels to each defense-news item. The labels are a category
          (procurement, operations, policy, technology, industry), an
          operational domain (air, land, sea, cyber, space, multi), and a
          region (indo-pacific, europe, middle-east, africa, americas,
          global).
          <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Full writeup &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-contract" data-term="contract seam interface api schema">
        <p class="tag">SYSTEMS</p>
        <h3>Contract seam</h3>
        <p>The boundary where one service depends on another through a
          stable request/response shape. The point is to make integration
          explicit instead of relying on hidden assumptions.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> I froze two of these across
          repositories (SYS-004 for <code>POST /classify</code>, SYS-006 for
          <code>GET /notes</code>) and wrote tests on both sides of each.
          SYS-005 is the asynchronous writeback loop over the same
          <code>/classify</code> call, so SYS-004 guards its only cross-repo
          hop. I described all of that as &ldquo;contract tests in CI&rdquo;
          and said a renamed field would fail the build. An audit of my own
          decision log in July 2026 showed that was wrong.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>What was actually true:</strong> each side
          asserted against its <em>own</em> copy of the shape. When the
          classifier added a third field, the provider&rsquo;s test fixture was
          updated in the same commit, and the consumer&rsquo;s test kept
          passing against a stub it wrote itself. Both suites stayed green
          through a breaking change. Two unit tests that happen to agree
          aren&rsquo;t a contract test &mdash; a real one needs a single shared
          artifact both sides check against, so changing one copy fails the
          other&rsquo;s build.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>What I did about it:</strong> SYS-018
          landed on 2026-07-18. Each provider now generates one schema from its
          live response model and commits it, so the file cannot describe a
          shape the service does not return. Every consumer fetches that file
          in its own CI and fails on divergence. The
          <code>/classify</code> schema is closed, which makes an added field a
          detectable break. The <code>GET /notes</code> schema stays open,
          because the consumer reads a subset and an added field must not
          redden its build. One gap stays open on purpose: a consumer that
          cannot fetch the schema warns and exits 0, so a real provider outage
          reads as a pass. <a href="projects/the-system.html#decision-contracts">Full decision &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-eval" data-term="eval evaluation eval harness eval-driven development testing">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>Eval / eval harness</h3>
        <p>A scored test set with known-correct answers, used to check
          whether a change actually helped &mdash; instead of trusting that
          it reads better.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> this is the discipline
          the whole site is built to demonstrate. Every prompt change,
          retrieval experiment, and model choice got measured against one,
          including the changes that failed and got reverted.
          <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html#why-the-numbers-can-be-trusted">Full decision &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-f1" data-term="f1 precision recall macro-f1 accuracy metrics">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>F1 / precision / recall</h3>
        <p>The numbers I use to score the classifier instead of trusting a
          skim. <strong>Precision:</strong> of everything I labeled X, how
          much was actually X. <strong>Recall:</strong> of everything that
          was actually X, how much I caught. <strong>F1:</strong> the balance
          of precision and recall, so neither one alone can make the model
          look better than it is.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> see the classifier
          evidence table for the real numbers, synthetic vs. hand-labeled.
          <a href="index.html#work">Full table &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-false-green" data-term="false green check gate ci success verification">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>False green</h3>
        <p>A check that reports success without being able to fail &mdash;
          green whether the work happened or not. A check that cannot fail is
          indistinguishable from a check that passes.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> I audited my own repos and
          found six, including a mobile gate measuring unstyled pages and a
          counter logging zero denials off a key the log never carried. The
          diagnostic that catches them: what is this green measuring, and can
          it tell &ldquo;passed&rdquo; from &ldquo;never ran&rdquo;?
          <a href="projects/false-green.html">Full writeup &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-gold-set" data-term="gold set hand-labeled reference answer key">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>Gold set</h3>
        <p>A hand-labeled evaluation set used as the reference answer key.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> mine is small (54
          snippets) and pulled from real DoD news wire and SEC filings, not
          model-generated. Its job is to test label boundaries and eval
          discipline, not production-scale generalization.
          <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html#why-the-numbers-can-be-trusted">Full decision &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-idempotency" data-term="idempotency idempotent retry">
        <p class="tag">SYSTEMS</p>
        <h3>Idempotency</h3>
        <p>An operation that produces the same result whether it runs once
          or five times &mdash; safe to retry, no side effects pile up.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> it's why the
          classify-and-tag writeback didn't need Kafka's delivery
          guarantees. The tags I write are derived and idempotent, so
          re-running the step just re-arrives at the same state.
          <a href="projects/the-system.html#decision-rightsized">Full decision &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-kappa" data-term="kappa cohen inter-rater agreement judge grading">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>Inter-rater agreement (Cohen's &kappa;)</h3>
        <p>How often two graders agree, after subtracting the agreement
          they&rsquo;d reach by chance. Raw agreement flatters graders on
          unbalanced sets; &kappa; doesn&rsquo;t.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> it&rsquo;s how I scored the
          faithfulness judge against my own labels &mdash; Opus at 0.762,
          Sonnet at 0.716, with overlapping intervals, so the data
          doesn&rsquo;t crown either judge. Note that &kappa; against one
          person&rsquo;s labels is agreement with that person, not
          inter-rater agreement in the sense this entry defines. Trusting a
          judge starts with measuring the judge.
          <a href="projects/faithfulness-judge.html">Full writeup &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-judge-model" data-term="judge model llm judge opus judge grading">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>Judge model</h3>
        <p>A separate model used to grade another model's output against the
          gold set, instead of grading it by hand at scale.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> I cross-check the
          classifier's real-text eval with an Opus judge &mdash; useful for
          catching errors hand-review can't keep up with, as long as I still
          spot-check the judge itself.
          <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html#why-the-numbers-can-be-trusted">Full decision &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-rag" data-term="rag retrieval augmented generation">
        <p class="tag">AI</p>
        <h3>RAG (retrieval-augmented generation)</h3>
        <p>Instead of asking a model to answer from memory, retrieve the
          relevant documents first, then hand only those to the model as
          grounding for its answer. Fixes the model's biggest weakness
          &mdash; it doesn't know your data &mdash; without retraining it.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> kb-agent is the RAG
          layer, retrieving from the knowledge base before it answers a
          query. <a href="index.html#work">Full system &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-recall-mrr" data-term="recall mrr retrieval rank hit rate search quality">
        <p class="tag">EVAL</p>
        <h3>Recall@k / MRR</h3>
        <p>Retrieval scores: recall@k asks whether the right document showed
          up in the top k; MRR asks how far down the list it sat. Together
          they say whether the retriever found it, and whether anyone would
          have seen it.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> kb-agent&rsquo;s retrieval
          eval runs on these. Filtering boilerplate out of the index lifted
          recall@1 from 0.630 to 0.741; the same harness then scored the
          hybrid-retrieval experiment as a wash, so dense-only stayed the
          default. The harness gets to say no.
          <a href="projects/kb-agent-retrieval.html">Full writeup &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision" id="term-tool-use" data-term="tool use structured output schema function calling">
        <p class="tag">AI</p>
        <h3>Tool use / structured output</h3>
        <p>Forcing a model to respond through a defined schema &mdash; a
          function call with typed, required fields &mdash; instead of
          parsing free text and hoping the format holds.</p>
        <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Here:</strong> my classifier's tool
          schema makes an invalid label a rejected API call, not a runtime
          bug. Out-of-enum responses were rare: once in 300.
          <a href="projects/defense-news-classifier.html">Full decision &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <script is:inline src="assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="assets/last-updated.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="assets/glossary.js"></script>
</Base>
